Terminoloy
~~~~~~~~~~

base device: this file/device/database holds all macroblocks, our worst case
threat-model assumes an adversary can make snapshots of it at will, each block
has a number

scubed3 partition: a block device whose contents are managed by a scubed3
deamon, writing to a scubed3 partition causes changes to the macroblocks in
the base device

macroblocks: the smallest bit of information to someone who doesn't have the
cryptographic key corresponding to it, a macroblock will either change randomly
(each bit changes with a probability of .5) or not at all

mesoblock: in a macroblock a series of mesoblocks are stored, along with
information on where the mesoblock belongs on the scubed3 partition.

indexblock: the first mesoblock in each macroblock contains information
about the other mesoblocks in said macroblock

ESSIV is a function that turns an uint64_t and two uint32_t's in a 128 bit
block for use as IV (see Encryption).

indexblocks are encrypted with IV = ESSIV(0, 0, macroblock_number)

mesoblocks are encrypted with IV = ESSIV(seqno, mesoblock_number,
macroblock_number), mesoblock_number is 1..255

the indexblock IV will repeat when the same disk block is written

the mesoblock IV will never repeat (as long as the seqno is not reset)

Encryption
~~~~~~~~~~

Every mesoblock (also the indexblock) is encrypted on its own with
CBC_ESSIV(AES256): AES-256 in CBC mode, the IV is ESSIV(iv0, iv1, iv2) =
AES-256 encryption, with SHA256(key) as key, of the 16 byte block made of iv0
(uint64_t), iv1 and iv2 (uint32_t), big endian.

An earlier design used a wide block mode (ABL) for the data, so that a
macroblock changes completely or not at all. That is not needed: every write
of a macroblock has a new seqno, so every data mesoblock has a new IV and
changes completely; the indexblock has a fixed IV, but its first 32 bytes are
a hash of the rest of it, which contains the seqno, so with CBC the whole
indexblock changes as well. To someone without the key, a macroblock changes
completely or not at all.

The cipher is given as MODE(BLOCKCIPHER) when a partition is opened or
created; scubed3ctl always uses CBC_ESSIV(AES256). The other modes, NULL (no
encryption) and CBC_PLAIN (CBC with the plain IV), are for testing only.

Threat model
~~~~~~~~~~~~

The adversary has full knowledge about the macroblocks (including history) and
may even have write access to them. This adversary may have keys to some
scubed3 partitions. The goal is to be able to plausibly deny the existance of
partitions to which the adversary has no keys.  Only paranoia level 3 protects
completely against this. We assume the adversary cannot detect read access.


Paranoia levels
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

When a new macroblock needs to be selected:

Level 0: not paranoid

it is selected from all allocated blocks based on it's emptyness, the most
empty block gets selected (the block with the least live data, at random if
there are more of them; not the block that is written now). The garbage
collector then copies the least data, so writing is faster, but the order
of the writes shows which blocks hold old data. A block with a lot of live
data is not rewritten as long as there are emptier blocks, so old versions
of discarded data in it stay on disk (they stay dead: their tombstones are
kept as long as they are on disk). Switch it on with
"paranoia 0" in scubed3ctl. After switching from level 0 to level 1 or 2,
the first two writes of every partition go to blocks that were chosen in
level 0 (they are chosen in advance, see "Write order").

Level 1: moderately paranoid

it is randomly selected from all allocated blocks (see "Write order" below).
This hurts write performance.

Level 2: just paranoid

every write goes to a uniformly random block of the whole base device,
whichever partition is written to. (this level of paranoia is required for
flash media, this kind of media keeps an internal record about the order in
which blocks are written to it, and for an adversary that makes many
snapshots) It is only safe if all scubed3 partitions are open in the scubed3
process: blocks that do not belong to an open partition are overwritten with
random data, so A PARTITION THAT IS NOT OPEN IS DESTROYED.

When a partition needs to write a block, the scheduler draws who writes,
until that partition is drawn: an open partition, with a probability
proportional to its number of blocks (it writes the block its juggler chose
as its next block, uniform over its blocks, see "Write order"; also if it has
no new data), or the free space (a uniformly random free block is overwritten
with random data, which looks the same as an encrypted block). So the written
blocks are independent and uniform over the base device, also while
partitions are resized (see "Write order"). The cost: a write
of a partition that has a fraction f of the blocks costs 1/f block writes on
average, and fsync waits for the turn of the partition.

Switch it on with "paranoia 2" in scubed3ctl, after opening ALL partitions
(it asks for confirmation, but not when switching between level 2 and 3:
then all partitions are still open); closing a partition switches back to
level 1 (its blocks would look free, a partition without blocks does not
matter). The level is not stored, after starting scubed3 it is level 1.
Creating, enlarging, shrinking and removing (shrinking to 0 blocks, see
"Shrinking") partitions is safe in level 2 and 3: a new partition is open,
and the blocks it gets were free.

Level 2 hides where the writes go, not how many there are: if you write only
to a hidden partition and reveal the key of another partition, the revealed
partition is seen to be rewritten without new data; level 3 hides that.
The same goes for the timing of the writes (level 3 hides that too): a partition writes its current
block when it is full, and the current block first takes the live data of
the next block, so the less live data the next block has, the more new data
fits before the next write. Someone who knows when you write data, can see
that the writes right after a pause more often go to blocks with little
live data (old blocks).

Level 3: extremely paranoid

as level 2, but the writes are done at random moments that do not depend
on what is written: on average RATE bytes per second (default 800 KiB/s, a
4 MiB block every 5.12 seconds), also if nothing is written; the time
between two writes is exponentially distributed (a Poisson process). At
every write, the scheduler of level 2 draws who writes: an open partition
writes its current block (with or without new data), or a free block is
overwritten with random data. So the number, the timing and the locations of
the writes do not depend on what is written (while scubed3 runs: when it is
started and stopped, and when the level changes, can be seen).

The cost:

- throughput: a partition with a fraction f of the blocks of the base
  device writes at most f*RATE (the garbage collector takes a part of it),
  so with the default rate a partition with 10% of the blocks writes at most
  80 KiB/s: DO NOT MAKE SMALL PARTITIONS if you want to use level 3
  (scubed3ctl shows the maximum of every partition when level 3 is switched
  on)

- waiting: a writer whose current block is full waits for the turn of its
  partition (on average macroblock size/(f*RATE)), so does fsync; while it
  waits, reads of that partition, writes to the other partitions and
  control commands wait too (except the paranoia command: you can always
  switch back)

- wear: RATE all the time; SSDs are rated for a number of terabytes
  written (TBW), typically a few hundred TB per TB of capacity:

	rate		block (4 MiB) every	per day		per year
	4096 KiB/s	1 s			362 GB		132 TB
	800 KiB/s	5.12 s			71 GB		26 TB
	400 KiB/s	10.24 s			35 GB		13 TB
	128 KiB/s	32 s			11 GB		4.1 TB

Switch it on with "paranoia 3 [KIBPS]" in scubed3ctl (in KiB/s, the default
rate is set with the option -r of scubed3), after opening ALL partitions;
closing a partition switches back to level 1. If the device cannot keep up
with the rate, it writes as fast as it can (writes that are more than 4
average intervals plus 1 second late are skipped).


How to start
~~~~~~~~~~~~

You need to provide a set of macroblocks to scubed3, to which it has read/write
access. Normally this is either a file or a block device, but it may be
anything. (you would, however, need to write a custom blockio_init_* function
and provide read, write and close methods to it if it is not a file or a block
device)

	scubed3 -b BASE [-a CONTROL_SOCKET] [-m MESOBLOCK_LOG] [-M MACROBLOCK_LOG]
		[-r KIBPS] [FUSE options] MOUNTPOINT

The mesoblock size is 2^MESOBLOCK_LOG bytes (12..20, default 14: 16KiB), the
macroblock size 2^MACROBLOCK_LOG bytes (at most 2^30, default 22: 4MiB), a
macroblock has 2 to 2^16 mesoblocks, and the index must fit in one mesoblock.
The sizes are not stored in the macroblocks: a set of macroblocks must always
be used with the same sizes. KIBPS is the rate of paranoia level 3 in KiB/s
(default 800), it does not switch level 3 on.


Key derivation
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

scubed3ctl derives the key of a partition from its passphrase with
PBKDF2-SHA256. The salt and the number of iterations are the same for all
partitions of a set of macroblocks (scubed3 cannot know which salt belongs
to which partition). They are read from the configuration file
/etc/scubed3.conf (another file can be given with -C):

	# scubed3 KDF parameters
	kdf=PBKDF2-SHA256
	salt=some random text
	iterations=16777216

Empty lines and lines starting with # are ignored, whitespace around keys and
values is ignored, all keys are optional. If the file does not exist, the
defaults are used: salt "scubed3_prod" and 16777216 iterations. The options
-s and -i override the values in the file.

The salt is not secret, but without it the partitions cannot be opened: keep
a backup of the configuration file (on paper is fine). A random salt can be
made with

	printf 'salt=%s\n' $(head -c 24 /dev/urandom | base64)

The configuration file reveals nothing more than the presence of scubed3: it
does not say how many partitions there are.

Control protocol
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

scubed3 listens on a unix socket, /run/scubed3/control (another path can be
given with -a, to scubed3 and to scubed3ctl). The directory of the socket is
created with mode 0700 if it does not exist; it must belong to the user that
runs scubed3 and nobody else may have access to it, otherwise scubed3 refuses
to start. So no other user can connect to the socket, not even for a moment,
and nobody can replace it. In addition, scubed3 only accepts connections from
root and from its own user. scubed3 refuses to take over the socket of
another running scubed3 process; a socket left behind by a crashed process
is replaced.

Commands can be written to the socket, and scubed3's response can be read. A
command is a line of text terminated with a linefeed '\n'.

A response starts with "OK\n", or "ERR\n". One or more lines follow.
The last line must be ".\n". This terminates the message.

The command scubed3ctl uses this interface to communicate with scubed3.

Commands (scubed3ctl has commands of its own, like create, open, resize and
mount, that use these; other commands are passed on as they are):

- open-internal NAME CIPHER KEY
- create-internal NAME CIPHER KEY

opens a partition, or creates a new one (of 0 blocks)

* NAME is the name of the file under the mountpoint, like root or swap, the
name has no real meaning, you can open any partition under any name

* CIPHER is the cipher, CBC_ESSIV(AES256) (see Encryption)

* KEY is the cipher key, base16 encoded (hex)

- close NAME

closes a partition (it must not be in use)

- resize-internal NAME BLOCKS RESERVED

resizes a partition to BLOCKS macroblocks, of which RESERVED are kept free
(the usable size is (BLOCKS - RESERVED)*255 mesoblocks); see Shrinking; a
partition has at least 2 blocks (with 1 block, every write would overwrite
the only copy of its state), 0 blocks removes it; at least 1 block is
reserved (the GC always empties one block, a partition that would be
completely full could not make progress)

- info NAME

shows the state of a partition: no_macroblocks, pending_removals, trims,
reserved_macroblocks, writes, no_indices, updated, wipe_left (a removed
partition: blocks that are not wiped yet), tombstone_slots,
tombstones_pending, mapped_mesoblocks

- p

shows all open partitions, the paranoia level, the number of free blocks
that were overwritten with random data, and the rate of level 3

- paranoia LEVEL [KIBPS]

sets the paranoia level (0, 1, 2 or 3, see Paranoia levels), and the rate
of level 3 in KiB/s; scubed3ctl has a command paranoia that asks for
confirmation (not between level 2 and 3), because level 2 and 3 destroy
partitions that are not open;
this command does not wait for a partition that waits for its turn

- check-data-integrity NAME

checks the hashes of all live mesoblocks of a partition, it reports the
number of mesoblocks checked, the number of corrupt mesoblocks, and the
corrupt parts (see Integrity) as "corrupt=OFFSET+SIZE" (in bytes, for at
most 100 mesoblocks)

- check-available NAME, set-aux NAME KEY VALUE, get-aux NAME KEY,
  set-close-on-release NAME BOOL, static-info

used by scubed3ctl (is a name free, remember the mountpoint, close the
partition when it is unmounted, mountpoint/size/version of scubed3)

- cycle NAME COUNT, verbose-juggler NAME

for testing: write COUNT blocks, show the state of the juggler

- help-internal, exit

lists the commands, closes the connection


Example calculation if indexblock size
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

For this example we assume the following. Macroblock size 4MB (m). Mesoblock
size 16kB (s). So, there are 256 mesoblocks per macroblock. Mesoblock
0 is the indexblock, the other ones are data blocks.

Whatever values are used, the indexblock MUST fit in one mesoblock! Required
size of the indexblock depends on the maximum amount of macroblocks scubed3 can
support (the size of a backing device can change, so scubed3 should be able to
deal with it, up to a maximum size).

The useful size of the device is
	(1<<(macroblock_log - mesoblock_log) - 1)(no_macroblocks - reserved_blocks)

Note that the usable size is limited by 2^32*(1<<mesoblock_log) - 1, because the
device is partitioned in mesoblocks, and we have 32 bits available to index
the mesoblocks.

Layout of macroblock:

0x000000 SHA256_HASH_INDEXBLOCK hash of 0x000020 - 0x003FFF
0x000020 32 bytes, MUST be 0
0x000040 uint64_t trim_floor (blocks with seqno < trim_floor are stale)
0x000048 24 bytes, MUST be 0
0x000060 uint64_t seqno
0x000068 uint32_t next_block (the block that will be written after this one,
         0xFFFFFFFF if none)
0x00006C 4 bytes, MUST be 0
0x000070 8byte literal "SSS3v0.4"
0x000078 uint32_t no_macroblocks
0x00007C uint32_t reserved_blocks
0x000080 uint32_t pending_removals (shrinking: blocks still to be removed)
0x000084 uint32_t no_trims (at most 8)
0x000088 trims[no_trims]: uint64_t seqno, uint32_t size (12 bytes each)
         a reference in a block with seqno < trims[i].seqno to a
         mesoblock >= trims[i].size is dead (discarded by shrinking)
......   rest of the 128 byte reserved space, MUST be 0
0x000100 uint32_t no_indices
0x000104 uint32_t idx0x01 (the logical mesoblock in data mesoblock 1, or
                           0xFFFFFFFE: mesoblock 1 holds tombstones, see
                           Discard)
0x000108 uint32_t idx0x02
......
0x0004FC uint32_t idx0xff
0x000500 uint64_t hash0x01 (hash of the ciphertext of mesoblock 1, see
                            Integrity)
0x000508 uint64_t hash0x02
......
0x000CF0 uint64_t hash0xff
0x000CF8 8 bytes, MUST be 0
0x000D00 bitmap: each bit represents a macroblock of the base device
		 0 FREE
		   this block may be ours: if so, it has our signature
		   on disk (otherwise we wouldn't be able to know that
		   it is ours) and we don't care about the contents of
		   this block
		 1 USED
		   this block is ours: it may not have our signature yet
		   because it has never been written and we care about
		   the data
		(notice that it only makes sense not to care about the contents
 		of a block if there are no older revisions of that data available
		that we DO care about...)
0x003FFC last 32 bits of bitmap (the bitmap is stored in units of 32 bits)
0x004000 mesoblock1
0x008000 mesoblock2
0x00C000 mesoblock3
........
0x3FC000 mesoblock255
0x400000 end

There is space to index 0x4000 - 0x0D00 = 0x3300 = 13056 bytes,
which corresponds to a base device size of 13056*8 = 104448 macroblocks,
which correspond to 4MiB * 104448 = 408GiB

Maximum usable size of the device is 2^32*(1<<mesoblock_log) = 64TiB, which is
sufficient in the light of the maximum size of the backing device.

In general the indexblock requires:
- fixed size: 260 bytes (including 128 bytes of reserved space)
- 4 bytes for every mesoblock in a macroblock (index)
- 8 bytes for every mesoblock in a macroblock (hash)
- 1 bit for every macroblock

Write order (the juggler)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The juggler keeps the pool of all blocks that currently belong to the
partition. After a block is written, the block that will be written next
is drawn uniformly from the whole pool (the block just written included)
and its number is stored in the index (next_block). So the sequence of
written blocks is independent and uniformly distributed over the pool, and
it does not depend on the data written.

The block that will be written next is called the tail. Before the current
block is written, it absorbs all live mesoblocks of the tail, so nothing is
lost when the tail is overwritten. If the tail is the current block itself,
the current block is written out without new data and the next block is
drawn again (this happens with probability 1/N for a pool of N blocks).

Paranoia level 2 is built on top of this: to write a block of the whole
disk, a partition is picked with a probability proportional to its pool size
(or the free space, with a probability proportional to its number of blocks),
that partition writes the block it has chosen as next_block. All writes on
the disk are then in uniform random order.

When the block that is drawn twice is the current block (it is its own
tail), it is written out empty at the next write of the partition, it takes
no data until then (one draw is one write, also in level 2); it removes no
blocks (shrinking) and carries no resize.

The location of a write is drawn one write in advance, from the pool as it
was then: the draw set of that write. The weight of a partition in level 2
is the size of the draw set of its current block (its next write lands
uniformly in it), so a resize keeps the writes on the disk uniform:

- a block that is added (grow) is put in the draw set of the next block: it
  becomes the next block with probability 1/(size of the draw set), as if it
  was there when the next block was drawn (this is safe: the new block is
  empty); it is not in the draw set of the current block, so in level 2 it
  belongs to the free space for one write of the partition (it may be
  overwritten with random data, it holds nothing of the partition yet)

- a block that is removed (shrink) stays in the draw sets it was in, it is
  given back to the unallocated blocks only when the next block is drawn
  without it (after the write that follows the write that records the
  removal); until then nobody writes it, just like the other blocks in the
  draw set that are not drawn

In level 1 the same holds within the partition: a write is uniform over the
pool as it was one write earlier (an added block can be written from the
second write after the grow on).

Integrity
~~~~~~~~~

The index holds a hash of the ciphertext of every mesoblock: the first 60
bits of its SHA256, the low 4 bits of the uint64_t mark corrupt parts of the
mesoblock. A mesoblock is divided in 4 parts of 4KiB, bit i is set if part i
is corrupt. The hashes are in the encrypted index, so someone without the key
cannot change a mesoblock (or put back an older version of it) and fix the
hash.

Every mesoblock that is read is checked against its hash. If the hash does
not match, all its parts are corrupt. Reading a corrupt part gives EIO.

When the GC copies a mesoblock with corrupt parts to the current block, the
corrupt parts stay marked as corrupt (in the new block the hash is valid
again, the mark keeps the corruption from being laundered).

Writing a corrupt part completely repairs it (as a disk remaps an unreadable
sector when it is written), so a filesystem that rewrites a 4KiB block can
recover. A write that covers a corrupt part only partially gives EIO, it
would need the corrupt contents.

The most recent block is checked completely on startup (see Durability), the
other blocks when they are read, and all live mesoblocks of a partition with
check-data-integrity.

A read error of the base device is handled the same way: the mesoblocks that
cannot be read are corrupt (the daemon keeps running). If the index of a block
of the partition cannot be read when the partition is opened, opening fails
(its contents are unknown, treating it as an empty block would lose data
silently). A write error of the base device is fatal: the daemon stops, what
was synced before is on disk (see Durability).

Reads and writes
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

A partition behaves as a file of fixed size: reading at or past the end
returns 0 bytes, a read or write that crosses the end is shortened, a write
that starts at or past the end fails with ENOSPC. Errors (EIO, see above) fail
the whole request.

A partition can be opened any number of times at once (resize2fs, for
example, opens it twice); the requests are handled one by one. It cannot be
closed or resized while it is open. The kernel tells scubed3 asynchronously
that a file is no longer open, so close and resize wait up to 3 seconds for
that before they report that the partition is busy. scubed3ctl refuses to
mount a partition that it has mounted already.

The size of a partition is changed with resize. Truncating it (open with
O_TRUNC, truncate(), ftruncate()) succeeds but changes nothing, as on a block
device: dd works without conv=notrunc, and an accidental "> partition" in a
shell destroys nothing.

Discard
~~~~~~~

When files are deleted, the filesystem knows which blocks are free, scubed3
does not: it would keep their data, and the GC would keep copying it. A
filesystem can tell, with discard (TRIM): run fstrim(8) on the mounted
filesystem now and then (or mount it with -o discard). The loop device turns
discard requests into fallocate(PUNCH_HOLE) on the partition, and write
zeroes requests into fallocate(ZERO_RANGE); both are supported, the range
reads as zeroes afterwards. Whole mesoblocks in the range are discarded, the
parts of mesoblocks at the edges are written with zeroes. This works for
ext3, ext4, XFS, btrfs, F2FS, vfat and every other filesystem that supports
discard; ext2 can be mounted with the ext4 driver (mount -t ext4) to get
discard, or the free blocks can be discarded offline with e2fsck -E discard.

A discarded mesoblock must stay discarded when the partition is opened
again, also if older versions of it are still on disk (in blocks that were
not written since). So a discard writes a tombstone: a list of discarded
logical mesoblocks in a data mesoblock of the current block (index value
0xFFFFFFFE; a big endian uint32_t count, followed by that many uint32_t's,
at most 4095 per mesoblock). When a block is replayed, its tombstones are
applied first: they discard the versions of those mesoblocks in older blocks
(the data in the block itself is newer).

A tombstone is needed as long as a version of its mesoblock is in the index
of a block on disk; scubed3 counts these versions for every logical mesoblock.
The GC carries tombstones that are still needed (from the tail, and from the
victims when shrinking) to the current block, as live data; the others die
out by themselves when the blocks with old versions are written again. A
mesoblock that is discarded while it is in the current block (not yet on disk)
is removed from it. A block that is added to a
partition (grow) may still have an old index of ours, that would be replayed
if the partition is opened again before the block is written: its versions
are counted, and they get tombstones if they were discarded.

The info command shows tombstone_slots (on disk), tombstones_pending (in the
current block) and mapped_mesoblocks (mesoblocks that hold data).

Shrinking
~~~~~~~~~

Shrinking sets pending_removals = no_macroblocks - target, the usable size
is computed from the target. Mesoblocks past the new end are obsoleted at
once, and a trim (seqno of the next write, new size) is recorded, so that
these references are never replayed, not even after a later enlarge. At
most 8 trims are kept; a trim is dropped when no block in the pool is older
than it, trim_floor is then raised to its seqno. A shrink that would need
a 9th trim is refused.

While removals are pending, every write also drains a victim: the block in
the pool (not the current block, not the tail) with the least live data.
Its live mesoblocks are absorbed by the current block (up to the brim);
when the victim holds no live data anymore, it is removed from the pool.
A removed block is FREE in the bitmap of the next written block; it is
given back to the unallocated blocks only after that block is on disk, and
only when the next block is drawn without it (see "Write order"), that is
after the write after it. A block that is its own next block removes
nothing: if the next write of the same block is interrupted, the state falls
back to the block before it, in which the removed block would still belong
to the partition. For the same reason, a resize first writes out a current
block that is its own next block. Closing a partition writes until its
removed blocks are given back. On startup, the blocks that the most recent
block removed are given back after the first write. A grow takes back the removed
blocks of the partition that are not given back yet, before it takes
unallocated blocks.
Removing a partition (shrinking it to 0 blocks): its last blocks (the
current and the next block, and the two most recently written blocks) are
removed together, when none of them has live data: by a final write of the
current block, with them FREE in its bitmap. The block written before it
also says that the size is 0 (if it is the current block, there is one more
write first).

Then the partition is wiped: blocks that still have an index of it would
bring it back, with its data, when it is opened again and its more recent
indices are overwritten (by random data in level 2 and 3, or by another
partition). All its blocks are overwritten with random data: the blocks it
removed, and the free blocks that still have an old index of it (from an
earlier shrink, they are found by reading the index of every free block).
The two blocks with its two most recent indices (last and prelast, they say
that the partition is empty) are kept until the others are wiped, then they
are wiped too. In level 0 and 1 this is done at once. In level 2 and 3 the
blocks are wiped when the scheduler draws them as free blocks, so the writes
stay uniform: a block that is not last or prelast gets random data, if last
is drawn its index is written again, if prelast is drawn the index is
written there and it becomes last (so there are always two indices that say
that it is empty: if a write is interrupted, the other one is left), when
only those two are left they get random data when they are drawn. That
takes long: about N*ln(k) block writes for k blocks on a base device of N
blocks. Until it is done, the partition stays open and cannot be closed or
resized ("p" and "info" show how many blocks are left), and its key cannot be
used for another partition. Switching to level 0 or 1 wipes it at once;
stopping scubed3 too. DO NOT USE THE PASSPHRASE OF A REMOVED PARTITION AGAIN
UNTIL IT IS COMPLETELY WIPED: a new partition with it would get lower
sequence numbers than the indices of the old one that are left.

A block of a partition that is being opened, that belongs to another open
partition (an old index of it is the most recent one that is left), means
that the state of the partition is damaged: it is not opened (the other
partitions are not affected).

A removed block is not written to by its partition: removing blocks causes
no extra writes and the write order stays uniform.

On startup, a block with our signature is stale (not replayed) if it is
FREE in the bitmap of the most recent block, or if its seqno < trim_floor.

Durability
~~~~~~~~~~

Every macroblock write is followed by fdatasync() of the base device, so
macroblocks reach the disk in the order in which they are written: the
block that absorbed the live data of its tail is on disk before the tail
is overwritten. Therefore only the most recently written block can be
incomplete after a power failure; on startup the hashes of all its
mesoblocks are checked, if one of them doesn't match, the block is ignored
(corruption of the most recent block by other causes looks the same, its
data is then lost). The index of the block before it
names the same block as next_block, so it will be written again and the
incomplete contents are overwritten.

fsync() on a partition writes out the current block (if it contains new
data), so after fsync() returns, everything written before is on disk.

Tests
~~~~~

The directory dev/ contains the tests and the development tools: torture
tests (grow, shrink, crashes, torn writes), tests with filesystems on top,
statistical tests of the paranoia levels and an independent offline reader
of the format; dev/README explains them.

Random block selection
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

How to randomly select blocks and assign roles to them?

In this example, we use 6 macroblocks, each write a group of 3 blocks needs to
be preserved. 

Suppose we have the random numbers 20312015..

The algorithm works as follows:
- assume the current list of random numbers is valid
- add a number
  * check for problems caused by this number, stop if no problems
  * mark problem causing number as unused, check for problems
    caused by this shift, of so continue here

step  | disk   | rng output | commentary
      | 012345 | 20312015.. |
---------------------------------------------------------------------
0     |   0    | 2          | check none, use empty #2 as DATA0
1     | 1 0    | 20         | check [0..0], use empty #0 as DATA1
2     | 1 02   | 203        | check [0..1], use empty #3 as DATA2
3     | 1302   | 2031 	    | check [0..2], use empty #1 as DATA3
4     | 1342   | 20312      | check [1..3], overwrite DATA0 at #2 with DATA4
5     | 5342   | 203120     | check [2..4], overwrite DATA1 at #0 with DATA5
6     | 5642   | 203_201    | check [3..5], conflict at #1 with DATA3, mark as unused
6.5   | 5642   | 2_3_201    | check [1..5], conflict at #0 with DATA1, mark as unused
6.4   | 5642   | __3_201    | check [0..4], conflict at #2 with DATA0, mark as unused
7     | 5642 7 | __3_2015   | check [], conflict at #2 with DATA0, mark as unused

So, how to know when a block designation as datablock is definitive (i.e.: will not be changed to _)?

Let's take for example (NO_BLOCKS=6, SIMULTANEOUS_BLOCKS=3)

012345012345

This looks pretty safe. Lets try to add numbers to break it

012345012345 ++ 145
0123450231451
012345023451 251
01234503425

